---
/**
 * Where the recognisable names land.
 *
 * The honest framing is load-bearing here. "Federer 329th" read alone is a
 * lie by implication: his actual gaps are about one percentage point, and he
 * is ABOVE expectation on serve and in tiebreaks. So the observed and expected
 * rates sit in the same table as the rank, and the caption states the size of
 * the effect before a single name appears.
 */
import { career, meta } from "@/lib/clutch/data";

const WANTED = [
  "Rafael Nadal",
  "Nick Kyrgios",
  "Pete Sampras",
  "Novak Djokovic",
  "Andre Agassi",
  "John Isner",
  "Roger Federer",
  "Andy Murray",
];

const rows = WANTED.map((name) => career.find((p) => p.name === name))
  .filter((p): p is NonNullable<typeof p> => Boolean(p))
  .sort((a, b) => a.rank - b.rank);

const pp = (v: number) => `${v >= 0 ? "+" : ""}${(v * 100).toFixed(1)}`;
---

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-head">
    <p class="text-kicker">Where the famous names land</p>
    <p class="text-note">of {career.length} ranked</p>
  </div>

  <p class="text-body measure mb-4">
    Read the columns, not just the rank. The gaps between what these players did
    under pressure and what the rest of their game predicted are
    <strong class="text-ink">one or two points in a hundred</strong> — this is a
    measure of small margins, not of nerve failing. Federer, for one, came in
    <em>above</em> expectation on serve and in tiebreaks.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="table table--tight">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="col-rank" scope="col">#</th>
          <th class="col-name" scope="col">Player</th>
          <th class="col-num" scope="col">Clutch</th>
          <th class="col-num" scope="col">Serve</th>
          <th class="col-num" scope="col">Return</th>
          <th class="col-num col-hide-sm" scope="col">Tiebreaks</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="col-rank">{p.rank}</td>
              <td class="col-name">
                <a class="link" href={`/player/${p.id}`}>{p.name}</a>
              </td>
              <td class="col-num font-bold">{p.rating.toFixed(1)}</td>
              {(["serve", "return", "tiebreak"] as const).map((k, i) => (
                <td class:list={["col-num", i === 2 && "col-hide-sm"]}>
                  <span class={p.c[k].r >= p.c[k].e ? "above" : "below"}>
                    {pp(p.c[k].r - p.c[k].e)}
                  </span>
                </td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="text-note mt-3">
    Columns are percentage points above or below what each player's own ordinary
    play predicted. Correlation between how good a player was and his clutch
    rating: <strong class="text-ink num">r = {meta.findings.qualityCorr}</strong> —
    near zero, so the index is not simply marking down the great.
  </p>
</section>
